.TH SPECTRAL 1 "2026-10-22" "spectral 0.9.0" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
spectral \- real-time noise suppression for PipeWire
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B spectral
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.br
.B spectral enable
.RB [ \-\-device
.IR ID ]
.RB [ \-\-threshold
.IR 0\-100 ]
.RB [ \-\-mic\-name
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-rumble\-filter
.IR on | off ]
.RB [ \-\-auto\-level
.IR on | off ]
.br
.B spectral
.RB { \-h | \-\-help }
.br
.B spectral
.RB { \-V | \-\-version }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B spectral
creates a virtual microphone that runs a chosen input device through RNNoise
denoising and voice\-activity gating, so any application can select the cleaned
microphone.
.PP
Run with no command it opens the graphical control panel. Run with a command it
talks to the background daemon and exits; everything but the voice test works
this way, without the GUI.
.PP
.B enable
starts the daemon if none is running, as the GUI does.
.BR disable ", " shutdown " and " status
report that none is running instead.
.B devices
reads the PipeWire registry directly and needs no daemon at all.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B enable
Create the virtual microphone and start denoising.
.TP
.B disable
Tear down the virtual microphone.
.TP
.B status
Show the daemon's state: device, mic name, threshold and toggles.
.TP
.B devices
List input devices with their current PipeWire ids.
.TP
.B shutdown
Stop the daemon.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Flags for enable
Every flag is optional except
.B \-\-device
on a first run, and an omitted flag keeps whatever the daemon already has: the
prior setting, or the default on a first run. That is what makes a bare
.B spectral enable
a "restore what I had" command.
.TP
.BI \-\-device " ID"
Input device to capture, as listed by
.BR "spectral devices" .
Required the first time; afterwards defaults to the remembered device.
.TP
.BI \-\-threshold " 0-100"
Voice gate threshold.
.B 0
means denoise only, with the gate off. Higher values gate more strictly on voice
activity.
.TP
.BI \-\-mic\-name " NAME"
Virtual microphone name. Only
.B [A\-Za\-z0\-9_\-]
is allowed, 1\-20 characters.
.TP
.BI \-\-rumble\-filter " on|off"
Cut low\-frequency rumble below roughly 90 Hz before denoising.
.TP
.BI \-\-auto\-level " on|off"
Even out voice volume automatically, with a peak limiter as the safety ceiling.
.SS First\-position options
Both are read in place of a command, not alongside one:
.B spectral \-\-help
prints the help text, while
.B spectral enable \-\-help
is a usage error.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help text and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a
.B disable
or
.B shutdown
that had nothing to do.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime failure, including
.B status
when no daemon is running.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; argument parsing failed before any command ran.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/spectral/state.toml
Persisted settings. Falls back to
.I ~/.config/spectral/state.toml
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, empty, or not an absolute path.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/spectral-daemon.sock
Control socket the GUI and the CLI use to reach the daemon. Spectral requires
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
to be set to an absolute path.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/spectral-daemon.log
The daemon's log. Attach it to a bug report.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/spectral/recordings/
Voice\-test recordings, readable by their owner only. Falls back to
.I ~/.local/state/spectral/recordings/
in the same cases as the settings file.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find an input device id:
.PP
.RS
.B spectral devices
.RE
.PP
Create the virtual mic on a specific device:
.PP
.RS
.B spectral enable \-\-device 62
.RE
.PP
Denoise only, with the voice gate off:
.PP
.RS
.B spectral enable \-\-threshold 0
.RE
.PP
Re\-enable with everything remembered from last time:
.PP
.RS
.B spectral enable
.RE
.SH NOTES
Device ids come from PipeWire and are scoped to the running PipeWire instance:
they change when PipeWire restarts, and a stale id will not resolve. The daemon
sidesteps this by also remembering the selected device by its node name and
re\-resolving from that, so a flagless
.B spectral enable
keeps working across reboots and PipeWire restarts. Re\-run
.B spectral devices
before passing an explicit
.BR \-\-device .
.PP
A Flatpak install exports the CLI as
.B page.codeberg.mmc.Spectral
rather than
.BR spectral .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pipewire (1),
.BR wireplumber (1)
.PP
Full documentation: <https://codeberg.org/mmc/spectral>
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at <https://codeberg.org/mmc/spectral/issues>.
